WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.234
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:09.571
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.871 --> 00:00:17.434
Unit twenty-one: Environmental History. Today you will describe how landscapes have been changed with the present perfect passive.

4
00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:20.282
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:24.408
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:26.437
Environment.

7
00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:31.969
Environment means the natural world in which people, animals and plants live.

8
00:00:32.583 --> 00:00:35.163
Human activity has changed the environment.

9
00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.449
Your turn. Say: Environment.

10
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.545
Environment.

11
00:00:43.283 --> 00:00:44.238
Landscape.

12
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.434
Landscape means the visible features of an area of land.

13
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.328
Rice fields dominate the landscape.

14
00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.557
Your turn. Say: Landscape.

15
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.575
Landscape.

16
00:00:59.317 --> 00:01:00.666
Deforestation.

17
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.346
Deforestation means the clearing of forests.

18
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:08.238
Deforestation has accelerated since 2000.

19
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.711
Your turn. Say: Deforestation.

20
00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:16.044
Deforestation.

21
00:01:16.783 --> 00:01:18.028
Agriculture.

22
00:01:19.017 --> 00:01:21.446
Agriculture means the practice of farming.

23
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.793
Agriculture still employs many Cambodians.

24
00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:27.943
Your turn. Say: Agriculture.

25
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.161
Agriculture.

26
00:01:32.917 --> 00:01:33.836
Climate.

27
00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.221
Climate means long-term weather patterns.

28
00:01:37.817 --> 00:01:39.990
The climate has become less predictable.

29
00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:43.154
Your turn. Say: Climate.

30
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:46.885
Climate.

31
00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:48.895
Human impact.

32
00:01:49.883 --> 00:01:53.369
Human impact means the effect of human activity on nature.

33
00:01:53.983 --> 00:01:56.447
Human impact on the Mekong is increasing.

34
00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.969
Your turn. Say: Human impact.

35
00:02:02.833 --> 00:02:04.043
Human impact.

36
00:02:04.783 --> 00:02:05.831
Resource.

37
00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:10.906
Resource means a supply of something useful, such as water or timber.

38
00:02:11.517 --> 00:02:13.714
Water is a precious resource.

39
00:02:14.317 --> 00:02:17.190
Your turn. Say: Resource.

40
00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:21.139
Resource.

41
00:02:21.883 --> 00:02:23.151
Sustainability.

42
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.821
Sustainability means using resources without damaging the future.

43
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:30.823
Sustainability must guide development.

44
00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:34.417
Your turn. Say: Sustainability.

45
00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:38.628
Sustainability.

46
00:02:39.433 --> 00:02:43.247
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:44.083 --> 00:02:48.219
Visal, how has the landscape around Tonle Sap changed in your lifetime?

48
00:02:49.317 --> 00:02:56.216
Many flooded forests have been cleared for agriculture, and fish catches have been reduced as a result.

49
00:02:57.317 --> 00:02:59.444
Is that only a recent problem?

50
00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:08.766
No. Landscapes have been shaped by people for millennia. Angkor's own forests were partly cleared to support its rice fields.

51
00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:11.466
And what about the Mekong?

52
00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:21.922
Its flow has been altered by upstream dams, so the annual flood pulse that has sustained the lake for centuries is weakening.

53
00:03:23.067 --> 00:03:24.497
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:28.046
Grammar focus: Present perfect passive.

55
00:03:28.346 --> 00:03:29.556
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:33.016
Many forests have been cleared for agriculture.

57
00:03:33.917 --> 00:03:36.926
The river's flow has been altered by upstream dams.

58
00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:40.524
Landscapes have been shaped by people for millennia.

59
00:03:41.467 --> 00:03:51.088
The present perfect passive (has/have been + p.p.) describes changes whose results are visible now, without naming who caused them. Add 'by + agent' when the cause is important.

60
00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:55.617
Be careful! Don't say: Many forests have cleared for farms.

61
00:03:57.567 --> 00:04:00.623
Say: Many forests have been cleared for farms.

62
00:04:00.923 --> 00:04:04.095
Present perfect passive: have been + p.p.

63
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:07.692
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.058
Nature Has a History.

65
00:04:10.358 --> 00:04:15.841
Environmental history studies the changing relationship between human societies and the natural world.

66
00:04:16.833 --> 00:04:21.921
Landscapes that look natural have often been shaped by centuries of farming, burning and settlement.

67
00:04:22.933 --> 00:04:29.229
In the last two centuries, however, the scale of human impact has been multiplied by industrial technology.

68
00:04:30.233 --> 00:04:36.981
Across Southeast Asia, large areas of forest have been converted into plantations of rubber, cassava and palm oil.

69
00:04:37.967 --> 00:04:44.401
Understanding these long-term changes helps societies design more sustainable ways of using resources.

70
00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:49.444
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:50.283 --> 00:04:52.991
Many rivers blank polluted by factories.

72
00:04:53.291 --> 00:04:56.724
A, have been. B, have. C, has been.

73
00:05:00.267 --> 00:05:02.162
The answer is A: have been.

74
00:05:03.317 --> 00:05:06.268
Large areas of forest blank into plantations.

75
00:05:06.568 --> 00:05:10.674
A, have been converted. B, have converted. C, has been convert.

76
00:05:14.233 --> 00:05:16.686
The answer is A: have been converted.

77
00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.745
The clearing of forests is blank.

78
00:05:20.045 --> 00:05:24.372
A, agriculture. B, deforestation. C, climate.

79
00:05:27.933 --> 00:05:30.293
The answer is B: deforestation.

80
00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:34.460
Using resources without damaging the future is blank.

81
00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:39.226
A, sustainability. B, human impact. C, landscape.

82
00:05:42.767 --> 00:05:45.323
The answer is A: sustainability.

83
00:05:46.483 --> 00:05:50.468
According to the reading, landscapes that look natural have often been…

84
00:05:50.768 --> 00:05:57.011
A, untouched by humans. B, shaped by centuries of human activity. C, created recently.

85
00:06:00.567 --> 00:06:04.157
The answer is B: shaped by centuries of human activity.

86
00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:08.321
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:09.183 --> 00:06:11.949
How has your local landscape changed over time?

88
00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:30.192
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:30.492 --> 00:07:05.649
My local landscape has changed dramatically over the past twenty years. Many rice fields near the edge of the city have been replaced by housing developments, factories and new roads. Several small ponds that used to collect rainwater have been filled in, so some streets now flood more easily. My grandparents say that the area was once surrounded by trees and birds. While development has brought jobs and better transport, I believe more green spaces should have been protected.

90
00:07:05.949 --> 00:07:07.809
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:08.717 --> 00:07:13.169
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and present perfect passive.

92
00:07:13.469 --> 00:07:17.607
Today's value is caring for nature. Care for plants, birds and our planet.

93
00:07:17.907 --> 00:07:20.641
See you in Unit twenty-two. Goodbye!

94
00:07:20.941 --> 00:07:23.813
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

